WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.079
"The ablations reveal how to use the method well.

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:08.496
Targeting a single metric in isolation gives the
largest improvement on that metric,

00:00:08.576 --> 00:00:13.976
while combining metrics achieves a balance, tracing
out a Pareto front of trade-offs.

00:00:14.056 --> 00:00:17.974
Combining front, side, and distance-to-reference
works nicely,

00:00:18.054 --> 00:00:21.391
but adding rear collisions causes a clear regression.

00:00:21.471 --> 00:00:25.969
The authors trace this to false positives in the
rear-collision metric,

00:00:26.049 --> 00:00:30.160
caused by log-replayed agents that do not react in
the simulator.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:32.223
Two hyperparameters also matter.

00:00:32.303 --> 00:00:38.478
The early-stopping budget K of the identification
policy: ten epochs works best for single-metric

00:00:38.558 --> 00:00:40.992
error sets and twenty for multi-metric.

00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:46.022
And the upsampling factor: performance improves up
to about a factor of fifty,

00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:49.117
after which side collisions start to rise again,

00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:53.630
echoing a similar saturation observed in the Just
Train Twice method."
